I'm afraid we have a disagreement on what constitutes 
"sleeping" and what constitues "resting."  It seems rather 
interesting that I can tell the game to sleep for <x> 
turns but not to rest.

de> Next time, try kipping near a teleport trap or a trapdoor, 
de> or just keep pressing the '.' key until you're fit to 
de> continue. If you want to play without applying any skill 
de> to the game, use the discovery mode. 

Uh huh, I get it.  We can ressurect all the old system 
interface debates (how do I know if I'm typing ahead, or 
I've lost a keystroke?  Do I hit ^R after every . press?) 
and all the old "I was resting and something came up and 
hit me eight times" complaints, which we've previously 
dealt with by saying "use 8s or 8.".  But we're never 
going to surmount the fact that Nethack simply does not 
operate, generally, in an environment that works well for 
skill games.  This is why, for instance, the idea to use 
shift-key for "aggressive moves" and let the normal keys 
represent movement only gathered so much support; having a 
deliberately frustrating interface is *not* the way to 
make a fun game.  It's not, in most circles, considered a 
particularly enjoyable challenge to, e.g., circumnavigate 
the fortress without having a finger slip and plunge you 
into the watery depths below.  There's simply no strategy 
involved there.  Of course, challenges of this sort cannot 
be entirely eliminated, but insofar as they are 
life-and-death, or they can be eliminated, that certainly 
doesn't make the game any less enjoyable.  It doesn't 
reduce exploration, and it doesn't reduce strategy.

de> The team have it right this time.

As you have no doubt guessed, I beg to differ.


